    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f7da6847_0de11463",
        "filename": "idsig/src/apksigv4.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 43,
      "author": {
        "id": 1415877
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-18T07:20:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Understood. But given that merkle tree is not that big, having it in memory won\u0027t be a problem. In practice, V4Signature created by V4Signature::create() holds the merkle tree in memory.",
      "parentUuid": "a4e461f5_03fa77e5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 43,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 43,
        "endChar": 12
      },
      "revId": "bf93df5ecc2651e416b17617395851b2a9b68005",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "58f9486d_a65d37cc",
        "filename": "idsig/src/apksigv4.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 43,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-18T07:46:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Merkle tree is about 1/127 of the file that it digests when SHA256 is used. So it could be big for large APKs which often can be few hundreds of MBs.\n\nI know that V4Signature::create holds the merkle tree in memory and wish to make it better. However, it\u0027s not a big problem as the memory will soon be reclaimed after the merkle tree is written to a file. Furthermore, the function is called in the host VM where memory is not a severe concern.\n\nFor V4Signature::from case, however, we really don\u0027t have to spend time and memory for reading the input file to fill the u8 array in the guest VM. Doing so will just slow down the boot process without no gain. We need to know only the offset of the merkle tree in the file. We then construct a loop device from the offset and pass the loop device to the kernel as the \u0027tree device\u0027. The actual read of the merkel tree will be done by kernel.",
      "parentUuid": "f7da6847_0de11463",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 43,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 43,
        "endChar": 12
      },
      "revId": "bf93df5ecc2651e416b17617395851b2a9b68005",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bff250ec_1ad6e0df",
        "filename": "idsig/src/apksigv4.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 43,
      "author": {
        "id": 1415877
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-18T08:05:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see. But still I don\u0027t understand why V4Signature needs to keep \"data\" in it. It makes its methods a bit hard to read. For example, write_into/merkle_tree should have \"\u0026mut self\" due to \"data\", which seems to be counter-intuitive. (I guess that it\u0027s the reason for not-deriving Debug for this struct). If V4Signature::from() is for guest side and we want to avoid reading the whole merkle tree there, then, how about making it explicit by not having \"data\" in V4Signature and V4Signature::create() returns (V4Signature, Vec\u003cu8\u003e (or MerkleTree?))?",
      "parentUuid": "58f9486d_a65d37cc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 43,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 43,
        "endChar": 12
      },
      "revId": "bf93df5ecc2651e416b17617395851b2a9b68005",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e39cfcfb_c03746b1",
        "filename": "idsig/src/apksigv4.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 43,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-18T09:29:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Merkle tree is conceptually a part of V4Signature. That\u0027s why we even have the method `merkle_tree` as a member of V4Signature (although that\u0027s only for testing for now). `create()` returning merkle tree as a separate value outside of V4Signature seem to break the abstraction.\n\nWhere in this CL `data` is causing a readability problem? I could fix that in a follow-up CL if possible.",
      "parentUuid": "bff250ec_1ad6e0df",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 43,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 43,
        "endChar": 12
      },
      "revId": "bf93df5ecc2651e416b17617395851b2a9b68005",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b05ff05f_b325b8d0",
        "filename": "idsig/src/apksigv4.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 43,
